  "full_response": "WebSockets and HTTP long polling are both techniques for achieving real-time communication between a client (such as a web browser) and a server.\n\nWebSockets provide a full-duplex communication channel over a single ongoing connection, which means that data can flow in both directions simultaneously. When you establish a WebSocket connection, a bidirectional channel is created, and both the client and the server can send messages directly and asynchronously.\nWebSockets are well-supported in modern web browsers, and they allow for much faster more efficient communication compared to HTTP long polling. WebSockets can continuously transmit small messages without additional overhead or latency on each exchange, providing a more responsive and interactive experience for real-time apps such as gaming, chat systems, or stock quotes.\n\nOn the other hand, HTTP long polling involves the client sending an HTTP request to the server, which keeps the server holding onto that request until new data becomes available or a timeout occurs. If the client receives a response within a long waiting time, the server could simply reuse the holding connection and immediately send a response, allowing for quick updates on data being updated. While HTTP long polling can be useful, it requires making persistent connections and handshaking each time, which can be inefficient and time-consuming as compared to WebSockets.\n\nIn short, WebSockets provide a more real-time, bidirectional, low-latency communication channel compared to HTTP long polling, while long polling can be a more viable option when working with older, slower, less efficient node.js web applications.",
